  // migrated-snapshot assertions that depend on directory state this sandbox does not carry, the
  // same class of pre-existing failure named in the 0.39.161 entry). PATCH bump per this file's
  // own rule -- bug fixes, no new API surface.] // [0.39.203: COS test environments with a real VM backend -- James: 'this? if not expand cos to fully fit idearium's and nexus' needs' (uploaded cos-standalone-windows-vm.zip). The upload is an older standalone extract of the same COS: its qemu-runtime.js is byte-identical to Nexus's, still with no guest command channel and no file sharing, and it lacks Nexus's newer cos/ci and advance-work-phase (it also carried a stray cli/powershell.exe, not imported). So COS itself was expanded. qemu-runtime: opt-in vmConfig.guestAgent (virtio-serial org.qemu.guest_agent.0 on a unix socket, tcp on Windows) and vmConfig.shareDir (9p -virtfs mount_tag cos_share on a Linux host, a vvfat drive elsewhere); buildQemuArgs returns qga and share endpoints; both default off so every existing VM compartment boots exactly as before (asserted); QEMU_BIN/QEMU_IMG_BIN exported. NEW cos/compartment/guest-agent.js: qemu-guest-agent client -- guest-sync (discards stale channel bytes and the 0xFF sentinel), guest-ping, guest-exec with captured output, guest-exec-status polling with base64 out/err decoding, agent errors thrown with the agent's own description, per-command timeout; connectWhenReady retries until the guest boots. NEW cos/testenv/index.js: one API, two backends, one result shape -- process (SandboxRunner, clean env, watchdog) and vm (ephemeral qcow2 overlay of a read-only base image, network none, the branch shared in over 9p and mounted at /mnt/cos, each target run in the guest via guest-exec, guest-shutdown + kill, overlay discarded so nothing persists); auto picks vm only when capabilities() finds qemu-system-x86_64 and qemu-img on PATH, a base image (COS_TESTENV_BASE_IMAGE) that exists, and a Linux host -- otherwise process, and the result always says which ran and why; vm requested but unavailable is refused with the reason, never silently downgraded. lib/repo-run.js now runs through cos/testenv (testBackend auto|process|vm) and reports backend + backendReason; Idearium route repo.run takes backend, new route repo.run.capabilities; the Run modal shows which backend ran and why. NEW tests/modules/test-cos-testenv.js 21/21: qemu args (guest agent, share, default unchanged), the QGA client over a real unix socket against a guest EMULATOR speaking the real QGA wire protocol (stale bytes, sentinel, exec/status/base64, agent errors, timeouts), capabilities, the vm backend end to end (spawned with agent + share, network none, share mounted before running, per-target results, shutdown + kill, overlay discarded), vm-unavailable refusal, and repo-run auto choosing process (with the reason) or vm. The guest is the only emulated part -- this machine has no QEMU and no guest image; a real boot needs QEMU plus a Linux base image with qemu-guest-agent, 9p and the runtimes installed. Mutation-checked: stale-byte sync guard removed -> crash; overlay discard removed -> 1 fail. repo-run 15/15 unchanged (now via testenv, process backend, reason reported). COS's own cos/test/test.js 156/157 -- the one failure (Stream: stamped event has seq) is identical at HEAD.]   // [0.39.202: any provider wears the repo hat -- James: 'the repo agent needs to be able to use chatgpt, gemini, deepseek as the agent wearing the hat. all outputs' / 'copilot routes to the provider, talks using a dom injector, then the dom mutator listening for the response.' That path already existed end to end and nothing set it: copilot /api/prompt with backend:'guardian' calls _lifeline.dispatchToNcpAgent -> guardian -> the provider's userscript (DOM injector in, mutation observer for the reply) and returns the real text; lib/repo-agent.js dispatch already forwarded backend/agent. NEW per-compartment provider in repo_agent_settings: providers() = auto + ollama + every guardian/userscript-<name>.js on disk (claude, chatgpt, deepseek, gemini, perplexity today; a new userscript appears with no code change); routeFor: browser provider -> {backend:'guardian', agent}, ollama -> {backend:'ollama'}, auto -> copilot decides. dispatch uses an explicit backend/agent, else an explicit provider, else the stored choice; unknown provider refused with the list. A provider is only a ROUTE: the hat persona, retrieval context, @learn protocol, inject protocol, learned observations, .inject writes and the agent-placement side question (which asks the SAME provider) are identical whichever model answers. Browser providers get 300s instead of 90s (a real tab must finish its reply); UI waits 320s. Replies carry provider/providerUsed; the transcript shows who answered. repo.agent.settings get/set extended with provider (+ providers list), prompt route takes body.provider. Agent tab: 'wearing the hat' select; CLI /provider [name] (documented in /help), provider in /status. NEW tests/modules/test-repo-agent-provider.js 35/35 on the real RepoLayer with a stub copilot recording each route: provider list equals guardian's userscripts; for chatgpt, gemini and deepseek each -- routes guardian -> that provider, same hat and protocols reach it, its stated-path code written into the compartment with exact bytes, its unlabelled block placed by asking the same provider, its @learn recorded, reply names the provider; per-message override beats the stored choice without replacing it. Mutation-checked: stored route not applied -> 11 fail. Regression 8 agent/idearium suites green. NOT DONE -- qemu for Run: COS's qemu-runtime boots a disk (qcow2, golden base + ephemeral overlay) but has no guest command channel (no qemu-guest-agent/guest-exec) and no way to put the repo into the guest (no virtfs/9p/shared drive), so it cannot run a test and return its exit code and output; it also needs qemu installed and a base image with the runtime inside. Not run against live provider tabs.]   // [0.39.201: Run/Test through COS -- James: 'use cos for the test environment. should have it all already.' It did; nothing here spawns a process itself. NEW lib/repo-run.js: BranchEngine.fork copies the repo's files into a branch owned by the repo's REAL COS compartment (the repo tree is never touched), SandboxRunner.run executes there (cwd = branch root, COS watchdog on runtime and output size), BranchEngine.recordRun keeps the result, and the branch is discarded unless keepBranch. Targets: explicit entry, else package.json main, else a conventional entry (index/main/server/app/cli .js, main/app/__main__ .py); mode test runs every *.test.js/*.spec.js/test_*.py/*_test.py (<=20), each its own sandbox run; runtime by extension via COS's RUNTIME_BINS names; nothing to run -> refused with the reason. COS change, opt-in: SandboxRunner opts.cleanEnv -- the child gets only PATH/temp/OS roots, never the parent's environment; default unchanged for every other caller. Previously the sandbox spread the full process.env, so agent-written code would have inherited Nexus's keys. Route repo.run (POST /api/repos/:uuid/run {mode, entry, timeoutMs<=300s, keepBranch}) wired into route table, interaction-contract and registry-components. The Run button is wired (no longer .tb-unwired): click runs, shift+click runs the tests; results in the diagnose modal per target (exit code, time, timeout/output-limit, stdout/stderr tail). test-repo-toolbar-mode updated for the wired Run (23/23). NEW tests/modules/test-repo-run.js 15/15 against the real BranchEngine and SandboxRunner -- the first test to exercise COS's sandbox at all: run in sandbox, parent secret does NOT reach the child, repo untouched, branch discarded, pass/fail with exit codes and stderr, hanging program killed by the watchdog, no compartment / nothing to run refused. Mutation-checked: cleanEnv removed -> the secret leaks -> 1 fail. HONEST LIMITS: process isolation, not a VM -- same OS user; dependencies are not installed (a repo needing node_modules must carry them, and the failure is shown); COS's qemu runtime is the stronger boundary if ever needed. Not run on a booted Nexus.]   // [0.39.200: no md files, only node types -- James: 'no md files. only node types.' The file-tree plan is no longer a PLAN.md chunk in the project: it is data on the manifest (fileTree.files) and a .filetree NODE (new registered type, lib/node-schemas/schema.filetree REAL, written by lib/file-tree-plan.js writeTreeNode at idearium/data/nodes/filetree/<specUuid>.filetree) carrying files by layer, bottom-up buildOrder, planSource, template and rejected count. File chunks are stored on disk under their real extension (agent-js-<id>.js), not .md; document-section chunks are unchanged. The file prompt reads THE FILE TREE from the manifest. FOUND BY REGRESSION: the first schema.filetree had an unquoted comma inside a YAML flow map; one unparseable schema made the tool registry unavailable and broke four unrelated suites (repo-context 10/6, repo-inject crash, repo-agent, injection-node-type) -- it only looked like a HEAD failure because git stash leaves untracked files in place. Rewritten block-style; schema.inject's flow-map descriptions quoted as well. New guard in test-file-tree-plan: every node schema (lib, guardian, ollama; 102) must parse as YAML -- mutation-checked with a broken schema -> 1 fail. test-file-tree-plan 52/52; repo-context 16, repo-inject 79, repo-agent 34, repo-agent-learn 33, repo-agent-node 44, repo-hat-memory 35, injection-node-type 4, snapshot-restore 30, file-versioning 33, import-baseline 25, ingest-stability 7, build-queue-poller 4, repo-graph 53, roadmap 39 all green; test-node-schemas NS-003/NS-012 unchanged.]   // [0.39.199: file tree first + COS templates in promote; chunk failures say why; stalled specs reported once. James: 'the file tree needs to be generated first with the list of files, the kernel, engine and runtime... all the templates from cos available for the promote to spec menu.' NEW lib/file-tree-plan.js: all 29 COS templates (18 archetypes, 11 blueprints) read LIVE from cos/archetype and cos/blueprint and offered in the promote picker (speceng.templates merges them; grouped archetypes / blueprints / spec-document templates); fromCosTemplate yields real files with {{name}} substituted, blueprints one directory per role with the role's stated purpose; the agent plans the full tree into kernel/engine/runtime/test via the same WARP dispatch the build uses, validated (safe paths, known layers, 80-file bound, duplicates rejected with reasons); template files win path clashes; two COS templates writing one path are refused; agent unreachable -> template files only with the reason, never an invented skeleton. spec-engine createFileTreeSpec: chunks ARE the files (realPath + file{path,layer,purpose}), PLAN.md and template files complete at creation with exact bytes, bottom-up order through the existing dependsOn gate (engine waits on kernel, runtime on engine, tests on runtime; edges by sectionId because _chunkDependenciesSatisfied resolves by sectionId -- a first version translated to chunk uuids and would have blocked every engine/runtime file forever, caught by reading the gate before testing); file chunks get a file prompt (layer, rules, plan, bounded lower-layer code already built) instead of 'section of a NEXUS component spec'. speceng.create branches to a file-tree spec for a COS template or fileTree:true; document specs unchanged. Picker: 'plan the file tree first' on by default. FIXED from James's boot log: (1) 8 chunks failed as 'axiom/cascade failure after 3 attempt(s)' with no reason -- runCascade recorded every attempt's provider and error but warp/dispatch/index.js collapsed it to a count; now carried as attemptLog (count unchanged for existing callers) and the chunk error names each provider's reason; (2) a spec whose unfinished chunks were all terminally failed was re-triggered every 15s forever, printing the same two lines ~40 times in 10 minutes -- the poller now reports once per distinct terminal state and stays quiet until a chunk changes. tests/modules/test-file-tree-plan.js 46/46 on the real COS registries, real spec-engine and real WARP cascade; mutation-checked (layer ordering, file prompt, path validation). Regression 20 suites green; test-node-schemas NS-003/NS-012 unchanged from HEAD. Not run against a live model.]   // [0.39.198: the agent works out where its code goes -- James: 'the agent's fucking job is to understand it.' v0.39.197 only injected blocks whose fence stated a path and punted everything else back to the person as 'unaddressed', and it asked James to decide whitespace. Wrong division of labour: the agent has the atlas, the chunk index and the turn's context. lib/repo-inject.js fromReply() now resolves every block in order of evidence and records how on the node (source.addressedBy + evidence): fence (the agent stated the path), symbols (the block defines top-level symbols that exist in exactly ONE file per the repo's own chunks/index.json -- a match across two files is never guessed between, unless exactly one was in the context the agent was shown this turn), agent (still ambiguous or new: the agent itself is asked, on a side session <sessionId>-resolve with channel idearium-repo-agent-resolve so the question never lands in the conversation, given the original request, the block, its symbols and the repo's real files as candidates; it answers a path, NEW <path>, or NONE). The person is never asked where code goes. Shell/console blocks are commands, not files -- reported, never written, never worth a round trip. An agent answer that escapes the tree still goes through _safeRelative and is refused; an unreachable agent is reported as unresolved, never hidden. INJECT_PROTOCOL rewritten from a rule into a hint (stating the path is fastest; otherwise you will be asked). WHITESPACE decided, not deferred: source code needs its exact bytes, so injects write with preserveWhitespace -- threaded as an OPT-IN through RepoLayer.writeTextFile(opts) -> writeFile(opts) -> spec-engine completeChunk/addChunk, whose default stays trim-on, so every other caller is unchanged (snapshot-restore 30/30, file-versioning 33/33, import-baseline 25/25, roadmap 39/39, versionium-tab 32/32 all green). Inject read-back is exact again; normalized:true now only marks a file pre-dating this that an older path stored trimmed. Agent tab shows per write how it was placed (path stated / matched <symbols> / agent placed it), plus unresolved blocks with reasons and shell blocks as commands. tests/modules/test-repo-inject.js 62 -> 79, on the real RepoLayer: fence/symbols/agent placement, the agent asked only for what evidence could not place, candidates drawn from the real index, NONE / escaping answer / unreachable agent, NEW path, byte-exact writes including trailing newline, the placement question on the side session carrying the original request. Two expectations of mine were wrong and fixed in the test, not the code: 'someone else's edit' goes through the default trimming path, as an ordinary caller's would. Mutation-checked: symbol match removed, agent placement removed, whitespace preservation removed (6 fail) -- all caught. Regression 18 suites green; test-node-schemas NS-003/NS-012 unchanged from HEAD. Not run against a real model -- how well a real model places code is unmeasured.]   // [0.39.197: .inject nodes + the full compartment agent CLI. James: 'we need .inject node with editor for the agent to build code for compartments, like the agents outputs go directly into the compartment automatically, maybe using the .agent agent id or hat id? need the full agent cli ui in the compartment repo agent tab that interacts with the hat and agent model.' NEW lib/repo-inject.js: an .inject is one proposed write of one file into one compartment's repo, authored by one agent (agentId = its hat uuid, the identity the .agent export already carries as _originUuid) or by the person, with its lifecycle on the node itself (proposed -> applied -> reverted, or rejected) and history of every event. NOT the existing injection / inject_rule types, which are both PROMPT-CONTEXT injection -- stated in the new schema.inject (REAL). Addressing: only a fenced block whose info string states a path becomes an inject, via guardian/lib/code-artifact.js's fenceDeclaredPath(), extracted verbatim from captureAll()'s inline regex so both use ONE rule (10.3; test-code-artifact 46/46 unchanged) through the same _safeRelative guard; a block with no path is reported unaddressed, never given an invented name. UNDO WITHOUT VERSIONIUM: each inject records before (exact prior content, or null if it created the file); revert writes it back or deletes. CONFLICTS REFUSED, NOT MERGED: apply refuses if the file changed since baseSha, revert refuses if it changed since what landed; both overridable with force, which is recorded in history, and the UI asks for the word 'force' typed. Writes go through the ONE real repo write path -- the RepoLayer is passed in, never re-implemented -- and every apply/revert READS BACK through it. MODES per compartment: review (default; agent code waits as proposed injects) and auto (agent code is written on arrival -- James's 'directly into the compartment automatically' -- each write still an inject that reverts). FOUND BY THE TEST, not by reading: RepoLayer.writeFile does not pass spec-engine's preserveWhitespace, so completeChunk applies its documented default .trim() (spec-engine BYTE-FIDELITY 2026-07-14) and 'x;\\n' lands as 'x;'. That shared default is NOT changed here (it governs every other caller); the read-back accepts exactly that rule and nothing looser, the node records normalized:true and appliedSha256 so the difference is visible, and revert's conflict check pins to what actually landed rather than to the proposed bytes. OPEN: whether repo writes should preserve whitespace is a real decision for every RepoLayer caller, not settled here. DISPATCH: lib/repo-agent.js compose() carries INJECT_PROTOCOL (only with a hat); dispatch accepts the caller's layer and turns addressed blocks 